<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ronald Reagan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ronald Reagan was the 40th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1981 to 1989, after careers as a film actor and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. He cut taxes, increased military spending and took a hard line against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, launching the Strategic Defense Initiative, but in his second term negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> and signed the INF Treaty of 1987, which eliminated a whole class of nuclear missiles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan.jpg" alt="Ronald Reagan" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Evans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Portrait_of_President_Reagan_1981.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…United Nations repeatedly condemned apartheid, and in 1973 the General Assembly adopted a convention declaring it a crime against humanity; sports, cultural and consumer boycotts and disinvestment campaigns spread across Western countries, and the United States Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act of 1986 imposed sanctions over President <b>Ronald Reagan</b>’s veto.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1985 foreign banks refused to renew South Africa’s loans, and in 1986 the United States Congress passed the Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act over the veto of President <b>Ronald Reagan</b>, while European and Commonwealth countries also imposed sanctions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The post-war boom ended in the late 1960s and early 1970s with stagflation, and monetarism gained prominence under Margaret Thatcher (1979–1990) and <b>Ronald Reagan</b> (1981–1989), shifting attention from Keynes’s collectivist concerns to individual choice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>After the economic crisis of the 1970s, Margaret Thatcher and <b>Ronald Reagan</b> turned from Keynesian economics to what became known as neoliberalism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Minutes after <b>Ronald Reagan</b> was sworn in, Iran freed the Americans held since the seizure of the US embassy in Tehran in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Birthplace of Ronald Reagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in 668 St. Cloud Road, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.63,-89.79&amp;year=1971" aria-label="Birthplace of Ronald Reagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2362 680 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ronald Reagan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2812 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2812 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.63,-89.79&amp;year=1971">What was here in 1971? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
